<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-greens">
  <title>Green's functions</title>

    <p>
      We're now going to use the delta function to probe the solutions to differential equations. Just like the action of a matrix can be reduced to its response to elementary basis vectors, the action of the differential operator in a boundary value problem can be reduced to its response to unit impulse functions, which we can think of as a sort of <q>basis</q> for the set of forcing functions, in the sense that just like
      <me>
        \mathbf{v} = \sum_{i=1}^n v_i e_i
      </me>
      for vectors in <m>\R^n</m>, we have
      <me>
        f(x) = \int_a^b f(\xi) \delta(x - \xi)\, d\xi
      </me>
      for integrable functions <m>f</m>. 
    </p>

  
</section>